A||N||6||CountFiero||Timing Gear replacement cost?||||5||
Z||000000||CountFiero||11-04-2001||05:54 PM||||My '84 2.5 stopped dead the other night. Cranks fine, all electrics (radio lights etc.)work fine.  But no spark and no gas.<BR>I thought it was the Ignition Mod. (again)<BR>--yes, I know use Delco next time, BUT it appears that it is the Timing Gear that is the culprit.  Took off the Dist.cap and cranked the engine.  The Rotor did NOT turn.<BR>From what I have been reading here this is NOT uncommon at about 120K.  Mine has 135K<BR>(100K I put on myself). Aftermarket gear sets seem to run about 50 to 65 bucks (cheap)<BR>but from what they say the labor is the killer.  I'd try it myself but after reading<BR>the description in Haynes manual it seems like a HUGE job, with alot of potential for error.  Finally the real question.  What is<BR>this job going to cost if I let the "professionals" do it?  The answer may be<BR>scarey enough to get me to try the job myself.<BR>If I do try it, any tips from anyone who has done it on this engine?<BR>Thanks---I LOVE this site, and my Fiero. The<BR>kids call it the "cool car".||||reg||5
Z||000001||JohnF||11-04-2001||06:24 PM||||I just had it done about 2 months ago.  Cheap replacement of parts - expensive labor intensive changeover. My '88 2.5 with Delco replacement, at a Fiero knowlegeable shop with lower than average hourly rates, about $500.00.<P>Be aware that the metallic aftermarket set will probably last longer than 120K but will be noisier the whole 15 - 20 years they last!<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||11-05-2001||08:29 PM||||Before you do this.... take off the oil filler cap and look for valve rocker motion. That will tell you for sure if it's the cam gear. (Distributor could also be the gear on the distributor shaft came loose.)<P><BR>Gear type is a trade off...<P>metal, done right, will probably last longer.<P>Phenolic is dimensionally more stable than a metal driven gear. This allows less lash between the gears and thus more consistant valve performance over the entire operating range.<P>I don't know what people a whining about anyway.... 100,000 plus miles out of a timing set isn't bad. I think most people are just used to chains that get all sloppy long before they actually brake. At least Fiero's 4 cyl doesn't peen the valves over when the gears quit. (Unlike Ford Escort which was known for snapping timing belts at about 40-50,000 miles and wrecking the head when it did.)<P>The gears can be replaced with engine in the car... One set of tools and procedure is a <A HREF="http://www.pontiacfiero.com" TARGET=_blank>www.pontiacfiero.com</A> <P>GM also put out a TSB with a procedure. It's available to the dealer network or thru <A HREF="http://www.alldata.com/" TARGET=_blank>ALLDATA</A>.<P>The GM puller amounts to a good grade steering wheel puller. The "press" is a suitable bolt, nut and heavy washer.<P>Neither method is easy, but they are better than pulling the cam, which create problems of it's own...<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">   <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT><p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 11-05-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||WKDFIRO||11-05-2001||10:05 PM||||I would have to totally disagree with JohnF about the noise of a metal gear. When my 87 finally gave up a bearing at over 250K miles I decided that I wanted to go with a metal gear. I had seen the hell that is trying to get that gear off and decided I could live with the noise if my luck decided to give out on the non-metal gear. <P>What I found is that the metal gear is no louder than the regular gear. I still drive it daily and could tell you that I don't know if I could tell an Iron Duke with metal and one without.<P>I would be interested to find out if anyone else with the metal gear noticed the same thing. ||||reg||14
Z||000004||CountFiero||11-05-2001||10:33 PM||||I actually got up the courage to ask several local garages for an estimate on this notorious repair.  Both were within 25 dollars of each other, at $1050.00  I didn't believe it either.  Labor was quoted at $70.00 / hour, and about 11 and a half hours to complete the job.  HUH???  I know it's supposed to be a pain-in-the-*** job but over a Grand???   Kinda like the time I took it in to have the brakes done at Midas.  They quoted me $1,500.00 because the parking cable was fouled up.  I have been dinking with cars since I was 13 and I've never seen quotes like they are making these days...absurd.  I guess they know that they have the general public over a $20K barrel with the "new" car option, so quoting a $1K repair doesn't even make them blush any more.<BR>THANKS to everyone for their input so far, and as to the contraversary about metal or standard timing gears it looks as though that debate will go on for a LONG...time.<BR>---OGRE---I'd be interested in hearing a more detailed description about the "pressing" on technique for the newly installed gears ,if you get time.  I know that you must be one of this sites MOST over worked contributors.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||9
Z||000005||theogre||11-06-2001||12:20 AM||||In short, very short... I don't have the TSB handy. This is just an overview. Do Not take it as complete and try to work from it.<P>Once you free things up, lower the back of the cradle some, and remove the right splash shield, balancer, timing cover, etc... Maybe even take out the motor mount for more engine drop....<P>The GM TSB instructs you to drill and tap a specific size hole in the cam. This is your "pressing" hole.<P>Then drill and tap 2 holes in the gear. In this case they match the bolts for your steering wheel puller. (Use a good one like Lisle # 45000.) These holes get punched in the gear hub close to the cam. (You hit the cam with the drill or tap and your SOL.)<P>You intall the puller and yank the gear.<P>Then you use a bolt, nut, and some washers to press on the new gear using the hole you previously drilled and tapped in the cam.<P>I'm certain I've left stuff out but this gives you an idea of what's involved. The TSB tells you what to set for runout and things. (Get the runout too far off you'll probably damage something...)<P>Before you actually try this, subscribe to ALLdata DIY for a year and get the actual GM TSB that explains the whole mess. It's around $25 for a year. If the online service will print... buy plenty of paper, ink, etc, and save all the 84 specific stuff that isn't in the Haynes book. Especially the wiring...<P>It's not rocket science but it is time consuming. PFKB estimates 12 hours if I remember right...||||reg||1
Z||000006||JohnF||11-06-2001||01:29 PM||||You are right about the "noise" battle of metallic sets.  FYI - I pulled the bill on my replacement, it is a follows:<P>Timing Gear Set $ 74.80<BR>Gsk Set         $ 19.44<BR>Labor           $360.00<P>Total           $454.24<P>(labor also included changing plugs and new oil and filter installed).<P>This rate was $65.00/hour - so about 5 hours for the timing chain.<BR>||||reg||12
